hy does matrix power correspond to the operator viewpoint?","options":["Powers raise each matrix entry to the $k$-th power.","Powers mean composing a map $V\\to V$ with itself.","Powers are defined only for invertible matrices.","Powers can be taken for every rectangular matrix."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a4eab6d1dd09808adbacfaa2","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_hq_b3_linear_operator_010_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A linear operator on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is written as $\\boldsymbol{x}\\mapsto\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{x}$. Which size can the matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ have?","options":["$2\\times2$","$3\\times2$","$1\\times2$","$2\\times3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8d0d12120015debf285f5aac","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_hq_b3_linear_operator_014_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{P}\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}^2$, $\\boldsymbol{P}(x_1,x_2)=(x_1,0)$, and $\\boldsymbol{Q}\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$, $\\boldsymbol{Q}(x_1,x_2)=x_1$. Which statement is correct?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{P}$ is a linear operator on $\\mathbb{R}^2$; $\\boldsymbol{Q}$ is a linear map but not an operator.","Both are linear operators on $\\mathbb{R}^2$, since both are linear maps defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$.","Neither is an operator, since both throw away the second coordinate.","Neither is linear, since both send the nonzero vector $(0,1)$ to zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d4050429bd17dc6b136c12f6","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_hq_b3_linear_operator_016_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{D}\\colon p\\mapsto p'$ be the derivative operator on the vector space of polynomials. What is $\\boldsymbol{D}^2(x^3)$?","options":["$6x$","$3x^2$","$9x^4$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f3820f725f042b32ba186dd8","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_hq_b3_linear_operator_020_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}\\colon V\\to W$ be a linear map. Which condition guarantees that $\\boldsymbol{A}(\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{v})$ is defined for every $\\boldsymbol{v}\\in V$?","options":["$W\\subseteq V$","$V\\subseteq W$","$\\dim W=\\dim V$","Linearity of $\\boldsymbol{A}$ already suffices, whatever $W$ is."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d2332fba757943ad0ad387d6","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_linear_operator_3a7642d2_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which of the following maps is a linear operator on $\\mathbb{R}^2$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{T}\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}^2$, $\\boldsymbol{T}(x_1,x_2)=(x_1+1,\\,x_2)$","$\\boldsymbol{T}\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$, $\\boldsymbol{T}(x_1,x_2)=x_1-x_2$","$\\boldsymbol{T}\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}^2$, $\\boldsymbol{T}(x_1,x_2)=(x_1x_2,\\,0)$","$\\boldsymbol{T}\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}^2$, $\\boldsymbol{T}(x_1,x_2)=(x_2,\\,2x_1-x_2)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a28abe4d4bc67e90df630332","time_limit":25}]}}